# React Project — AI Development Pipeline

## Project Stack
- **Framework**: React (with hooks, functional components)
- **State Management**: Context API / Redux Toolkit (check `src/store` for actual setup)
- **Styling**: Vanilla CSS with design tokens
- **Testing**: Jest + React Testing Library
- **Linting**: ESLint with project rules (see `.eslintrc`)
- **Package Manager**: npm / yarn (check `package.json`)

## Coding Standards — Always Enforce
- Functional components only; no class components
- TypeScript types/interfaces for all props and state (if TS project)
- Named exports preferred over default exports for components
- Custom hooks in `src/hooks/` with `use` prefix
- API calls abstracted into `src/services/` or `src/api/`
- No inline styles; use CSS modules or utility classes
- Accessibility: semantic HTML, ARIA labels where needed
- No `any` types in TypeScript
- Avoid `useEffect` for derived state — use `useMemo`
- Keep components under 200 lines; extract sub-components freely
- All async operations must handle loading and error states

## AI Pipeline Slash Commands
The following slash commands are available and each triggers a specialized agent:

| Command   | Agent File                        | Purpose                              |
|-----------|-----------------------------------|--------------------------------------|
| `/plan`   | `.cursor/agents/plan-agent.mdc`   | Branch setup + planning a ticket     |
| `/replan` | `.cursor/agents/plan-agent.mdc`   | Re-plan after requirement changes    |
| `/develop`| `.cursor/agents/develop-agent.mdc`| Implement an approved plan           |
| `/test`   | `.cursor/agents/test-agent.mdc`   | Generate & run tests                 |
| `/review` | `.cursor/agents/review-agent.mdc` | Code review against plan & standards |
| `/document` | `.cursor/agents/document-agent.mdc` | Layperson `documentation.md` per ticket |
| `/prdoc` | `.cursor/agents/prdoc-agent.mdc` | PR title + body → `pr-description.md` |
| `/impact` | `.cursor/agents/impact-agent.mdc` | Branch impact vs `develop` |
| `/reldoc` | `.cursor/agents/reldoc-agent.mdc` | Roll up ticket docs → `ai-doc/PROJECT_CONTEXT.md` |

## `ai-doc/` — consolidated context for agents

The **`ai-doc/`** directory holds **project-wide, token-efficient context** for AI agents (see `ai-doc/README.md`).

- **`ai-doc/PROJECT_CONTEXT.md`** — generated by **`/reldoc`**; merges per-ticket `documentation.md` files into one hierarchical overview so agents can orient without reading every ticket folder.
- **`ai-doc/<ticket-number>/documentation.md`** *(optional)* — per-ticket docs may be mirrored here; **`/document`** still writes to **`ai-dev/<ticket-number>/documentation.md`** by default unless the team copies or syncs into `ai-doc/`.

Prefer **`PROJECT_CONTEXT.md`** for broad orientation; use **`ai-dev/<ticket>/`** for the live plan, control board, and implementation trackers.

## Ticket Folder Structure
All AI-driven work lives under `ai-dev/<ticket-number>/`:
```
ai-dev/
  <ticket-number>/
    plan.md             — Architecture, scope, assumptions, task slices
    control.md          — Status tracking for all pipeline phases
    development.md      — Task-by-task implementation tracker
    testcases.md        — Test results and stats
    documentation.md    — Document all code changes done for PR with testing steps
```

## control.md Status Values
| Phase       | Possible Statuses                          |
|-------------|--------------------------------------------|
| planning    | `planning` → `needs-clarification` → `done` → `approved` |
| development | `not-started` → `in-progress` → `done`    |
| testing     | `not-started` → `in-progress` → `failed` → `done` |
| review      | `not-started` → `in-progress` → `done`    |

## Agent Behaviour Rules (Global)
1. Always read `control.md` before acting on any command
2. Never skip a gate check (e.g. `/develop` requires planning status = `approved`)
3. Always update `control.md` after completing any phase
4. Stash messages must follow: `[AI-PIPELINE] <ticket> — auto-stash before branch switch`
5. ⛔ **CRITICAL RULE**: Do not do `git commit` or `git push` and do not even assume HUMAN has asked it in any manner. It should only be used by the defined agent (`/commit-approve`) with its specific defined command.
6. Always pull latest `develop` before creating a feature branch
7. Branch naming: **exactly** `<ticket-number>` (e.g. `POS-1` — no `feature/` prefix or slug)
